----- stdout -----
Show a note drawn for the terminal.

The note's frontmatter, then its Markdown with headings, emphasis, lists, tables, quotes, callouts and highlighted code styled, laid out at the terminal's width. A note taller than the screen goes through `$PAGER`, or `less -R` when it is unset; `-n` never pages. Without a terminal on stdout the text comes without escape sequences, and `NO_COLOR` leaves out the colours. Must resolve to exactly one note, like `render`.

Usage: ntropy show [OPTIONS] [ID|PATH|QUERY]...

Arguments:
  [ID|PATH|QUERY]...
          A full ULID, a note's filename or path, or a query DSL expression, as trailing arguments that are each read on their own; words in one argument form a phrase (omitted = choose from all notes).
          
          A lone argument that ends in `.md` or `.age` and names an existing file selects the note in it: a bare filename is looked up in `all-notes/`, any other path is taken as given, and a symlink to a note, such as a view entry, selects that note. A file that is no note of the vault is an error, and so is the name of a note file that does not exist. Any other argument ending in `.md` or `.age` is a query.

Options:
      --vault <PATH>
          Operate on the vault at this path (overrides all other resolution)

      --width <COLUMNS>
          Lay the note out at this many columns instead of the terminal's width (80 without a terminal)

  -n, --non-interactive
          Force non-interactive (plain) behavior even on a TTY

      --strict
          Treat malformed/badly-named notes and skipped views as errors instead of warnings

  -i, --identity <PATH>
          Use this age identity file instead of the OS credential store.
          
          `$NTROPY_IDENTITY` is consulted when the flag is absent.

      --passphrase-file <PATH>
          Read the vault passphrase from the first line of this file.
          
          Supplies the passphrase wherever one would otherwise be typed: creating an encrypted vault, unlocking one, or converting one. On `vault passphrase` it is the *current* passphrase.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

